qx|A1|st||rh||ah|Board 1 IMPs   -    March 20, 2024|pn|hondo717,coxenrider,cfrleon,Honeydog3|
md|3SJ8HAQJ5DQCQJ8742,SKT95HKDAT7532CT6,SA732HT8632D6CK93,SQ64H974DKJ984CA5|sv|0|
mb|P|mb|P|mb|1C|mb|1D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|1@D is fine and probably best. 2@D is another option, though a possible spade fit might be missed.|
PG| ***** 1 ***** |


mb|1H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let us discuss North's options over 1@D. North could make a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=negativedoubles,Negative Double^*N|CP||FP||at|, which in this specific auction (a 1@D overcall over a 1@C opener) promises both majors, usually 4-4. |

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 2 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With 5-4 in the majors I usually prefer to bid my 5-card major, particularly if it is hearts (because partner still has the option of bidding 1@S). However, with North's weak hearts, I think double is slightly preferable to 1@H, although 1@H is certainly possible.|
PG| ***** 3 ***** |


mb|3d|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|3@D, even using  |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| is possible but I think East is too strong to bid 3@D. In this cycle 3@D typically shows a weak hand.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 4 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East should cue bid to show a stronger raise. In this occasion, since the opponents have bid 2 suits (clubs and hearts) East has the choice of two different cue bids--2@C and 2@H.|
PG| ***** 5 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Here 2@C shows a 3-card limit raise and 2@H shows a 4+ card limit raise--East has 4 diamonds. Therefore, I would bid 2@H with the East hand.|
PG| ***** 6 ***** |


ub||
pg| ***** 7 ***** |


mb|2H|mb|D|an|was going to bid 2H|
pg| ***** 8 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My double is ^*BStolen Bid^*n here, meaning I intended to bid 2@H.|
PG| ***** 9 ***** |


mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West has a pretty nice hand. The weakest bid West can make is 3@D. The strongest bids are redouble and pass shows a decent hand. I like redouble best with the nice 6-4 distribution.|
PG| ***** 10 ***** |


mb|R|MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Since I have shown likely 4 hearts, North should bid 3H as a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| bid.|
PG| ***** 11 ***** |


mb|3H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With a 10 card fit, 4@D is a reasonable call again based on |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| although it is fairly unusual to bid 4 of a minor in a competitive auction. Here it is possible.|
PG| ***** 12 ***** |


mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now I have a problem to bid. I love my 6-4 distribution and singleton diamond, though have many quacks. Further, E/W might be making 5@D and not sure we are making 4@H. If I knew they were not making 5@D I would bid 4@H. So pass appears to be the better choice.|
PG| ***** 13 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I don't think I would bid 5@D with West hand but it might work out.|
PG| ***** 14 ***** |


