up|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 4@H we lose 1 spade, 1 club, and 1 diamond for sure. In theory I can drop the @HK and make 4@H. However, in practice we have a heart loser for down 1. This hand illustrates the problem with the double dummy analyzers that are in common use.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Double dummy analyzers will tell you that 4@H makes, but if played correctly 4@H would be down 1 because there is no reason to drop the singleton @HK without peeking. The finesse is the correct play. |
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Some of you may be familiar with the ^*BRabbi's Rule^*n in bridge which states ^*B"When the singleton king is offside, play the ace^*n”, but obviously that is satirical and is impossible to apply in real life.|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|This is one example of why you need to take the double dummy analyzers with a grain of salt.|
PG| ***** 27 ***** |


